WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:04.140
Hey Crazies.

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.500
I have received a challenge.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:10.940
So I’ve been thinking some crazy thoughts
and, that is, what would it take to artificially

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:15.540
generate a magnetic field strong enough to
protect the Martian atmosphere?

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:20.500
And that requires some crazy physics, so where
else do you go but the craziest people in

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:22.580
physics and, that is, in the Science Asylum.

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:26.960
You know, Phillip, if we do this right, it
might not actually be as crazy as it sounds.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.780
First, let me explain the problem with Mars.

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:33.180
It has a really thin atmosphere and it's
very cold.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:37.600
It ranges from about 400 pascals up to about
900 pascals.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.180
That’s pretty low compared to Earth’s
atmosphere.

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:42.160
Temperatures aren’t any better.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:48.499
They range from about -130 degrees Fahrenheit only up to about
-20 degrees Fahrenheit

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:50.520
It’s basically an ice planet.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:56.860
The atmosphere is also 95% carbon dioxide
and there is no liquid water on the surface.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:02.519
As you can see from this fancy phase-diagram,
liquid water is physically impossible under

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:03.940
Mars’s normal conditions.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.340
Anyway, none of this is great for humans.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:10.500
I mean, your eyes aren’t going to pop out
of your face or anything, but it will definitely

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.160
kill you and quickly.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:14.060
It wasn’t always like this though.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:16.760
As Phillip mentioned:
When it comes to terraforming Mars, one of

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:21.460
the major problems is the fact that Mars has
a very thin atmosphere because it has essentially

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:22.940
no magnetic field.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:27.440
Back in the day, it had a magnetic field,
which means it had a thicker atmosphere, which

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.980
means it had liquid water on its surface.

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:36.140
It’s just that about 4.2 billion years ago,
the magnetic field turned off, the atmosphere

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:39.980
stripped away, and the water either froze
or evaporated.

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:43.340
And as Phillip pointed out:
If we go through all this effort of generating

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:46.380
an atmosphere, we don’t want it to be blown
away by the solar wind.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.480
Which, you know, sounds reasonable.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:49.800
Let’s do this!

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.600
But Mars doesn’t have the gravity of Earth,
so it can’t...

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:53.940
You’re right. You’re right.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:55.320
We’ll deal with that first.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.280
There’s another problem with Mars.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.960
It only has about 38% of Earth’s surface
gravity.

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:01.880
The upside?

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:04.020
You can jump 2.6 times as high.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:05.060
The downside?

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:08.620
It can only hold on to about 38% as much atmosphere
as Earth.

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:13.460
We can’t fix the surface gravity problem,
so the highest the pressure could ever get

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:15.480
is 0.38 atmospheres.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:18.060
That’s roughly the pressure near the peak
of Mt Everest.

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:22.550
It’s not enough to walk around outside without
an oxygen mask, but it’s definitely enough

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:23.670
to get some liquid water.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:24.800
Now let’s do this!

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:26.210
OK, let’s see.

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:28.080
Elon Musk wants use nukes.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.080
We’d have to drill down to the Martian core
and detonate the nukes there.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:34.920
That would re-liquefy the core and get it
moving again.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.180
But that seems like a lot of energy.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:40.540
Considering the core’s current temperature
and pressure, which would give us a density,

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:41.910
specific heat, and latent heat.

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:46.140
Allowing us to calculate mass and, ultimately,
thermal energy, which comes to approximately

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.020
10 to the 29 joules!

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:54.800
That’s about 10 trillion megatons or 100s
of billions of the largest nuke ever created.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:56.200
I don’t think this is going to work.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:01.220
Even if we could manage to do this somehow,
there’s no guarantee the liquid core would

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:03.100
actually generate a magnetic field.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:06.480
and, even then, we’d have to keep the core
from cooling off again.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:07.740
We need a better option.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:09.960
Hmm.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:11.300
I’ve got it.

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:15.220
Who says this magnetic field has to be created
by the planet or even on the planet?

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:17.420
Let’s just put a big magnet in space.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:22.980
Unfortunately, we can't just put this thing into orbit around Mars because we need it to always be

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:25.020
between Mars and the Sun.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:26.860
We need a Lagrange point.

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:31.220
There are 5 points in and around a circular
orbit that are stable.

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:34.660
Other objects can sit in them and they’ll
all orbit together.

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:38.240
Any deviation and the object gets tugged back
into place.

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:41.200
They’re very useful for human-made probes.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:44.610
For our space magnet, L1 will do quite nicely.

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:49.620
L1 for Mars is about 160 Mars’s away from
Mars.

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:51.600
That looks something like this.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.080
It’s really far away.

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:57.600
So we’d need it to be strong enough that
Mars would still be inside of it.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:03:59.380
Which isn’t actually that difficult.

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:04.260
Earth’s magnetic field is only about 30-60
microteslas and the Moon is inside it.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:06.180
Remember how far away the Moon is?

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:10.200
This I don’t even need to calculate because
NASA scientists already did it!

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:17.100
Dr. Jim Green and a team of planetary scientists
ran computer simulations for a space magnet at L1.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:23.140
According to them, a magnet anywhere from
50-500 microteslas should do the trick.

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:25.620
We can easily generate that with current technology.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:29.940
If we did this, Mars’s atmosphere would
thicken over time on its own because it’s

00:04:29.941 --> 00:04:32.290
not getting blasted away by the solar wind.

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:38.000
The northern polar ice cap is a bunch frozen
water covered in a thin layer of dry ice.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.780
As the atmosphere thickens, the temperature
will rise, and that ice cap will melt.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:47.860
First, the dry ice, releasing more CO2, a
known greenhouse gas.

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:51.920
This will speed up the warming process even
more, which will help melt the frozen water

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:52.930
underneath.

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:56.250
Eventually, liquid water would be able to
flow on the surface.

00:04:56.250 --> 00:05:01.850
As much as 1/7 of Mars’s ancient ocean could
come back, making Mars look like it did in

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:03.060
the good-old-days.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:07.340
Again, there still won’t be enough pressure
to, you know, breath, but it’ll make colonizing

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:09.280
Mars a heck of a lot easier.

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:12.880
So I’ve got one thing to say to you NASA:
Get your [crow noise] to Mars.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:14.400
Got any questions about Mars?

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:15.760
Please ask in the comments.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:17.280
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:19.680
Don’t forget to subscribe if you’d like
to keep up with us.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:24.380
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to
be a little crazy.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:28.360
The featured comment comes from Bill DeWitt,
who suggested an alternate name for Spin:

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:29.140
Aim!

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:30.720
Angular Intrinsic Momentum.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:32.300
It's aimed up or down.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:33.060
You know.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:34.380
That’s not bad!

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:37.120
I think I’ll propose it at the next Science
Insanity Meeting.

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:38.580
Anyway, thanks for watching!

